 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **View Directions:** If the scalar field depends on view direction (e.g., standard NeRF), extracting a static isosurface is invalid because the sigma value changes with the camera position. The code substitutes fake viewdirs pointing to the origin, which produces incorrect geometry [S1, unofficial].
-*   **IJK to XYZ Interpolation:** Interpolating intersection points in IJK space and then transforming to XYZ is an approximation. For strictly uniform grids, this is exact. For non-uniform structured grids, it introduces geometric error, though often negligible [S2].
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The algorithm assumes the grid covers the entire volume of interest. Vertices on the boundary of the grid may result in open meshes if not capped [S2].
+*   **View-Dependent Fields:** If the scalar field depends on view direction (e.g., standard NeRF), running Marching Cubes directly is invalid because the sigma value changes with the camera. Substituting fake view directions (e.g., pointing to the origin) may produce incorrect geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **IJK-to-XYZ Interpolation:** The method of computing intersections in IJK space and then transforming to XYZ is not technically correct for non-uniform grids, but the difference is negligible for most applications [S2].
+*   **Ambiguities:** Standard Marching Cubes has topological ambiguities on cube faces and inside the cube. These are often resolved using extensions like the Asymptotic Decider or Matveyev's Algorithm [S3].
 
