 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the grid. Each voxel is processed independently [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the scalar field and the output mesh. The algorithm can be implemented as a streaming operation requiring only two slices of data in memory at any given time, allowing it to work on extremely large datasets [S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with resolution. Parallelization is straightforward due to cell independence [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; robust; produces watertight meshes for well-behaved scalar fields; streaming capability allows handling of large volumes [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Accuracy is limited by grid resolution; artifacts may occur near boundaries or with ambiguous topologies (e.g., saddle points) without advanced disambiguation; view-dependent fields cannot be accurately extracted as static meshes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a quick, robust mesh extraction from a regular grid is needed, and the grid resolution is sufficient to capture the desired detail.
-*   **When not to use:** When high geometric accuracy is required at low resolution (consider [Dual Contouring and Surface Nets](6319-dual-contouring-and-surface-nets.md)); when the scalar field is view-dependent; when the grid is unstructured (consider [Unstructured and Arbitrary Grid Methods](11103-unstructured-and-arbitrary-grid-methods.md)).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the grid. Each voxel is processed independently.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the scalar field storage. The output mesh size depends on the surface complexity but is generally proportional to the surface area.
+*   **Performance:** The algorithm is highly parallelizable as each voxel is processed independently. It is one of the most common visualization methods in use today due to its simplicity and speed [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, fast execution, well-understood topology (with extensions), works on any structured grid.
+*   **Limitations:** Topological ambiguities in the original table (resolved by extensions), fixed resolution (aliasing if grid is coarse), IJK-to-XYZ interpolation approximation [S2].
+*   **When to use:** When a quick, approximate surface mesh is needed from a regular grid, or as a baseline for more advanced methods.
+*   **When not to use:** When high geometric accuracy is required without post-processing (use [Smart Conversion](7489-smart-conversion.md) or [Dual Contouring](6319-dual-contouring-and-surface-nets.md)), or when the grid is unstructured (use [Unstructured and Arbitrary Grid Methods](11103-unstructured-and-arbitrary-grid-methods.md)).
 
 ## Background
-The algorithm was originally developed and patented by GE Medical Systems for use in their CT and MRI equipment. The patent has since expired, placing the algorithm in the public domain [S2]. It is one of the most common visualization methods in use today [S2].
+The algorithm was originally developed and patented by GE Medical Systems for use in their CT and MRI equipment. The patent has since expired, placing the algorithm in the public domain [S2]. It is widely used in medical imaging, scientific visualization, and 3D printing. Variants and extensions have been proposed to address topological ambiguities, such as the Asymptotic Decider by Nielson and Haman, and Matveyev's Algorithm in 1999 [S3].
 
